.page renice 1 change the scheduling priority of running processes

# SYNOPSIS
*renice* [*-n*] _PRIORITY_ [[*-p*] _PID_...] [[*-g*] _PGRP_...] [[*-u*] _USER_...]

# DESCRIPTION
`renice` sets the nice value of processes that are already running to _PRIORITY_, a number from -20 (greedy) to 19 (polite). The identifiers that follow are process ids unless a switch changes what they mean: `-p` for process ids, `-g` for process group ids (every process of the group), `-u` for users (every process of the user, by name or number). The switches may be repeated. All threads of a process share its nice value.

Anybody may make their own processes more polite; only root may lower a nice value or change other people's processes. For every process `renice` prints the old and the new value.

_PRIORITY_ is the new value itself, not an increment. The leading `-n` is optional and only there for people who like to type it.

# OPTIONS
@ *-p*
    The following identifiers are process ids (the default).
@ *-g*
    The following identifiers are process group ids.
@ *-u*
    The following identifiers are users, by name or number.

# EXIT STATUS
0 if every priority was changed, 1 if any failed (the message names the process and the reason).

# EXAMPLES
    renice 10 -p 123 124
    renice -n 19 -g 77
    renice 5 -u reimu

# SEE ALSO
nice(1), ps(1), kill(1)
